name: sed
boss: Lookaround
flavor: I remember what you wrote. I match it again.
syntax: &&
brief: `sed` edits a stream. It reads a line, applies your commands, prints the
brief: result. The one you will use forever is `s/old/new/` -- and the trailing
brief: `g`, which changes every match on the line instead of only the first.
brief:
brief: `-n` turns off the automatic printing, which turns `sed` into a selector:
brief: `-n '5p'` prints only line 5. `-i` edits the file in place, and on a
brief: read-only root that only works on files you put somewhere writable.
brief:
brief: `&&` runs the next command only if the last one worked -- copy first,
brief: edit only if the copy landed.
---
q: {replace first @old@ per line of @cfg@ with @new@|swap the first @old@ per line of @cfg@ for @new@|change the first @old@ per line to @new@ in @cfg@}, with sed
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/' @cfg@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: s/pattern/replacement/ -- no trailing g means only the first match per line
---
q: {replace EVERY @old@ throughout @cfg@ with @new@|replace every @old@ with @new@ throughout @cfg@|swap every @old@ for @new@ throughout @cfg@}, with sed
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/g' @cfg@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: the g goes after the final slash
---
q: {replace @old@ with @new@ in @cfg@, ignoring case|replace @old@ with @new@ in @cfg@, case-insensitive|swap @old@ for @new@ in @cfg@, case-blind}, with sed
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/gi' @cfg@
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/Ig' @cfg@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: i (or I) after the slashes makes the match case-insensitive
---
q: %show% only line 3 of @log@ with sed, nothing else
a: sed -n '3p' @log@
out: WARN
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: -n suppresses the automatic print, so only what you p yourself appears
---
q: %show% lines 2 through 4 of @log@ with sed
a: sed -n '2,4p' @log@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: same p as line 3, but a range with a comma before it
---
q: %show% every @log@ line mentioning @sev@, using sed not grep
a: sed -n '/@sev@/p' @log@
out: @sev@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: a pattern in slashes acts as the address, selecting lines that match
---
q: {delete every line of @log@ mentioning @sev@|remove any line of @log@ that mentions @sev@|drop every @sev@ line from @log@}, with sed
a: sed '/@sev@/d' @log@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: d deletes the whole line the pattern matched, not just the text
---
q: {delete the # comment lines from @cfg@|remove every line of @cfg@ that starts with #|drop the # comment lines out of @cfg@}, with sed
a: sed '/^#/d' @cfg@
out: listen
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: ^# anchors the match to the start of the line
---
q: {delete the blank lines from @cfg@|remove every empty line from @cfg@|drop the blank lines out of @cfg@}, with sed
a: sed '/^$/d' @cfg@
out: timeout
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: ^$ matches a line with nothing between start and end
---
q: {delete the first line of @csv@, the header row|drop the header row from @csv@|remove line 1 -- the header -- from @csv@}, with sed
a: sed '1d' @csv@
out: ^gateway
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: a bare line number with d deletes just that line
---
q: {strip every leading space from the lines of @log@|remove the leading spaces from each line of @log@|delete whatever spaces open each line of @log@}, with sed
a: sed 's/^ *//' @log@
out: 2024
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: ^ *  matches leading spaces, replaced with nothing
---
q: {drop comments, then blanks, from @cfg@ in one sed pass|drop comments and blanks from @cfg@ in one sed invocation|in one sed pass, remove comments and blanks from @cfg@}
a: sed -e '/^#/d' -e '/^$/d' @cfg@
out: listen
ref: sed
teach: sed -e
hint: -e lets you give more than one script
---
q: {pull the port number out of @cfg@ using extended regex|extract the port number from @cfg@ with extended regex|using extended regex, pull the port number from @cfg@}, with sed
a: sed -E 's/^port = ([0-9]+)$/\1/' @cfg@
out: 8080
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -E
hint: -E gives you (groups) without backslashes; \1 is what the group caught
---
q: {pull the port from @cfg@ again with sed, using the older regex flag|repeat the port pull from @cfg@ with sed's older flag|redo the port pull from @cfg@ with sed's legacy flag}
a: sed -r 's/^port = ([0-9]+)$/\1/' @cfg@
out: 8080
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -r
hint: -r and -E are the same flag; -E is the portable spelling
---
q: {@csv@ is name,id,status,count -- with sed, swap name and id|@csv@ is name,id,status,count -- with sed, put id ahead of name|@csv@ is name,id,status,count -- with sed, reverse name and id}
a: sed -E 's/^([^,]+),([^,]+)/\2,\1/' @csv@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -E
hint: capture each field in a group, then reorder them with \1 and \2
---
q: {copy @cfg@ to edit.ini, then change @old@ to @new@|copy @cfg@ to edit.ini, then swap @old@ for @new@|copy @cfg@ to edit.ini, swap @old@ for @new@ in it}, with sed
a: cp @cfg@ edit.ini && sed -i 's/@old@/@new@/g' edit.ini
state: exists:edit.ini
ref: sed
teach: sed -i
hint: -i writes the file back rather than printing it
---
q: @log@ ends with why it died -- %show% that line, sed not tail
a: sed -n '$p' @log@
out: out of memory
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: $ means "the last line"
---
q: %show% @log@ from the first @sev@ to the end, with sed
a: sed -n '/@sev@/,$p' @log@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: a pattern address and $ together mean from the first match to the end
---
q: {save @old@-to-@new@ in s.sed, apply to @cfg@|write s.sed with @old@-to-@new@, run on @cfg@|put @old@-to-@new@ in s.sed, apply to @cfg@}, with sed
a: printf 's/@old@/@new@/g\n' > s.sed && sed -f s.sed @cfg@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed -f
hint: -f takes a filename holding the script, like -e takes it inline
---
q: {copy @cfg@ to edit.ini, swap @old@/@new@, backed up|swap @old@ for @new@ in a @cfg@ copy, backup kept|edit @cfg@'s copy @old@-to-@new@, backup kept}, with sed
a: cp @cfg@ edit.ini && sed -i.bak 's/@old@/@new@/g' edit.ini
state: exists:edit.ini.bak
ref: sed
teach: sed -i[SFX]
hint: a suffix stuck to -i means "back the original up under this name"
